%I A399192 #6 Aug 26 2026 23:07:14
%S A399192 0,1,2,2,3,3,4,4,4,4,5,5,6,6,6,6,7,7,8,8,8,9,10,10,10,11,11,11,12,12,
%T A399192 13,13,13,14,14,14,15,16,16,16,17,17,18,18,18,19,20,20,20,20,20,20,21,
%U A399192 21,21,21,21,22,23,23,24,25,25,25,25,25,26,26,26,26,27
%N A399192 a(n) = number of integers k <= n with g(k)^4 < k, where g(k) is the largest divisor d of k satisfying d <= sqrt(k) (A033676).
%C A399192 g(k)^4 < k holds if and only if k has no divisor d with k^(1/4) <= d <= k^(3/4), if and only if A006530(k)^4 > k^3, i.e., the largest prime factor of k exceeds k^(3/4). Proof: if p = A006530(k) > k^(3/4), every divisor of k is either a multiple of p (hence > k^(3/4)) or a divisor of k/p < k^(1/4). Conversely, if p <= k^(3/4) and p >= k^(1/4), then p itself lies in the excluded range; if all prime factors of k are < k^(1/4), multiplying them one at a time yields a divisor in [k^(1/4), k^(1/2)).
%C A399192 a(n) = A000720(n) for 1 <= n <= 15, since every composite k has a divisor in [2, sqrt(k)], so g(k) >= 2 and g(k)^4 >= 16 > k.
%C A399192 This is the m = 4 member of a family: for each integer m >= 3, the condition g(k)^m < k is equivalent to A006530(k)^m > k^(m-1), giving Sum_{c=2..floor(n^(1/m))} (A000720(floor(n/c)) - A000720(c^(m-1))) = a_m(n) - A000720(n). The case m = 3 is A396205. The equivalence fails for m = 2 (e.g., k = 12).
%C A399192 Limiting density: a(n)/n tends to log(4/3) = 0.28768... (follows from the formula together with the Prime Number Theorem).
%F A399192 a(n) = Sum_{k=1..n} [ g(k)^4 < k ], where g(k) = A033676(k) and [] denotes the Iverson bracket.
%F A399192 a(n) = Sum_{k=1..n} [ A006530(k) > k^(3/4) ].
%F A399192 For n >= 1, A000720(n) = a(n) - Sum_{c=2..floor(n^(1/4))} ( A000720(floor(n/c)) - A000720(c^3) ).
%F A399192 a(n) ~ n * log(4/3).
%e A399192 g(10) = 2 and 2^4 = 16 is not less than 10, so the indicator at k = 10 is 0; hence a(10) = a(9) = 4. (Note A006530(10) = 5 < 10^(3/4), so 10 is counted in A396205 but not here.)
%e A399192 g(22) = 2 and 2^4 = 16 < 22, so the indicator at k = 22 is 1; hence a(22) = 9.
%t A399192 g[n_] := Max[Select[Divisors[n], #^2 <= n &]];
%t A399192 Accumulate[Table[If[g[k]^4 < k, 1, 0], {k, 1, 71}]]
%Y A399192 Cf. A033676, A006530, A000720, A396205.
%K A399192 nonn,easy,new
%O A399192 1,3
%A A399192 _Eric Fodge_, Aug 21 2026
